<ui:FluentWindow x:Class="ChronoMock.App.MainWindow"
                 xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                 xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                 xmlns:ui="http://schemas.lepo.co/wpfui/2022/xaml"
                 xmlns:views="clr-namespace:ChronoMock.App.Views"
                 Title="{DynamicResource app.title}"
                 Width="1040"
                 Height="800"
                 MinWidth="1000"
                 MinHeight="360"
                 ExtendsContentIntoTitleBar="True"
                 WindowBackdropType="None"
                 WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
                 AllowDrop="True"
                 DragOver="OnWindowDragOver"
                 Drop="OnWindowDrop">
  <!--
    🔴 THE APP'S BACKGROUND GOES HERE, ON THE CONTENT ROOT, NOT ON THE WINDOW. A Background written on
    a ui:FluentWindow is accepted, looks exactly like a working one, and paints nothing: the window
    carried Background="{DynamicResource BrushBgBase}" for the whole life of this file and every pixel
    stayed at the wpfui default. Measured rather than reasoned - the attribute was replaced with a
    bright literal, the app rebuilt and screenshotted, and the images came back IDENTICAL BYTE FOR BYTE,
    zero difference across 1 872 000 pixels. Overriding the theme resources the FluentWindow style reads
    (WindowBackground, ApplicationBackgroundBrush) changed nothing either, by the same measurement.

    That silent nothing is what left the two halves of one app on two different colours: the calculator
    view looked right only because it carries its own root Grid background, which a Grid does honour, so
    the substitution panel and the window chrome sat on the wpfui #202020 while the calculator sat on our
    #1B1B1F. The seam was visible under the module switch.

    #1B1B1F is the palette's own base (Themes/Colours.xaml). It stays ours on purpose: the colour it
    replaced was a default of the dependency, so a wpfui release could have changed 86 percent of this
    window without anyone deciding it. The base is also the first rung of a four-surface ladder tuned
    together - a card separates from it at 1.14 contrast against 1.08 on the old colour, and the whole
    palette is slightly cool where #202020 is flat grey.

    Guarded by BackgroundGuardTests, which renders this window and asserts the painted colour, because a
    guard on the presence of the attribute would have passed happily on the dead one.
  -->
  <Grid Background="{DynamicResource BrushBgBase}">
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
      <RowDefinition Height="*" />
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <!-- The title bar carries the name, and beside the window buttons the one thing this application
         asks of anybody: a way to support it. It sits here rather than in the panels because both
         modules share this strip, so neither module has to give up a row to it, and it is the only
         place a control can be permanently visible without competing with the work. Support and About
         are shell chrome the phases never show, so they stay Click handlers on the window, not commands. -->
    <ui:TitleBar Grid.Row="0" Title="{DynamicResource app.title}">
      <ui:TitleBar.TrailingContent>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
          <!-- The tooltip says what pressing this does BEFORE it is pressed, and names the address. This
               application never opens a connection of its own, so handing a page to the browser is the
               one moment a user could feel that promise was broken - saying so first is what keeps it
               true rather than merely technically true. -->
          <Button x:Name="SupportButton"
                  Style="{StaticResource TitleBarActionButton}"
                  Click="OnSupportClick"
                  ToolTip="{DynamicResource support.tip}"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource support.name}">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
              <!-- The heart is decoration beside the word, never the whole message: it is not in the
                   automation name, and the label alone reads correctly if a font has no glyph for it
                   (zasady/13 9 - meaning never rests on one symbol). -->
              <TextBlock Text="&#x2665;" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource support.button}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                         Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Button>

          <!-- The way to the licence, the version and what is inside this build. It is a word rather than
               an "i" glyph because this strip already asks the eye to tell four small controls apart, and
               a labelled one is the only kind that survives that without a hover. -->
          <Button x:Name="AboutButton"
                  Style="{StaticResource TitleBarActionButton}"
                  Click="OnAboutClick"
                  Content="{DynamicResource about.button}"
                  ToolTip="{DynamicResource about.tip}"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource about.name}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </ui:TitleBar.TrailingContent>
    </ui:TitleBar>

    <!-- Module switch: the two halves of the product (substitution, calculator) share one window. The
         calculator is a separate view swapped into the content cell below. Meaning is carried by the
         label, never colour alone (zasady/13 9), and both options are keyboard-reachable radio buttons. -->
    <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceSm}">
      <RadioButton x:Name="ModeSubstitution" GroupName="AppMode" IsChecked="True" Checked="OnModeChanged"
                   Content="{DynamicResource nav.substitution}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}" />
      <RadioButton x:Name="ModeCalculator" GroupName="AppMode" Checked="OnModeChanged"
                   Content="{DynamicResource nav.calculator}" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}" />
    </StackPanel>

    <!-- Content cell: the substitution module and the calculator occupy the same cell, toggled by the
         module switch above (OnModeChanged flips the two containers' visibility). -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="2">

      <!-- 🔴 THE SUBSTITUTION MODULE IS NOW THREE PHASES that REPLACE each other, not one monolithic panel.
           SetupPhaseView while nothing has started, SessionPhaseView while a session is connecting/live/
           shutting down, ResultPhaseView once it has ended. Exactly one is visible: the view model's
           ShowsXPhase properties are mutually exclusive and keyed to the session STATUS, never IsIdle - a
           finished session goes idle again (the form unlocks to re-run) but its status stays terminal, so
           it must keep showing its result rather than snapping back to setup.

           Each phase inherits this window's SessionViewModel as its DataContext and binds Commands.X; the
           window hands those commands a shell (WindowShellInteraction) in code-behind for the picker,
           clipboard and confirm actions. Each phase also merges the parts library locally, and keeps doing
           so even though App.xaml now merges Parts application-wide: a {StaticResource PartX} resolves as the
           phase's BAML loads and never reaches Application.Resources. The application-wide merge is what
           gives the whole app the Parts look. -->
      <Grid x:Name="SubstitutionContainer">
        <views:SetupPhaseView Visibility="{Binding ShowsSetupPhase, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
        <views:SessionPhaseView Visibility="{Binding ShowsSessionPhase, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
        <views:ResultPhaseView Visibility="{Binding ShowsResultPhase, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}" />
      </Grid>

      <!-- Date calculator (chrono-mock 7.3), hidden until the module switch selects it. -->
      <views:CalculatorView x:Name="CalculatorContainer" Visibility="Collapsed" />
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
</ui:FluentWindow>
